{"id": "test-01", "text": "Grace Hopper programmed the Harvard Mark I during the Second World War and later helped design COBOL.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 12, "surface": "Grace Hopper", "qid": "Q11641", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 28, "end": 42, "surface": "Harvard Mark I", "qid": "Q776823", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 54, "end": 70, "surface": "Second World War", "qid": "Q362", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 95, "end": 100, "surface": "COBOL", "qid": "Q131140", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-02", "text": "Hubble showed that the Andromeda nebula lies far outside the Milky Way, using photographs taken at Mount Wilson Observatory.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 6, "surface": "Hubble", "qid": "Q43027", "ambiguous": true, "note": "Edwin Hubble (person): 'showed', photographs at Mount Wilson; popular reading (space telescope Q2513) is wrong", "wbs_rank": 4, "rest_rank": 2, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 23, "end": 39, "surface": "Andromeda nebula", "qid": "Q2469", "ambiguous": false, "note": "historical name of the Andromeda Galaxy", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 61, "end": 70, "surface": "Milky Way", "qid": "Q321", "ambiguous": true, "note": "galaxy, not the chocolate bar", "wbs_rank": 2, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 99, "end": 123, "surface": "Mount Wilson Observatory", "qid": "Q466863", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-03", "text": "Launched by NASA in 2009, Kepler found thousands of exoplanets using the transit method, which detects tiny dips in starlight.", "mentions": [{"start": 12, "end": 16, "surface": "NASA", "qid": "Q23548", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 2, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 26, "end": 32, "surface": "Kepler", "qid": "Q47272", "ambiguous": true, "note": "space telescope: 'launched', 2009; popular reading (Johannes Kepler) is wrong", "wbs_rank": 5, "rest_rank": 9, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 52, "end": 62, "surface": "exoplanets", "qid": "Q44559", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": null, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": false, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 73, "end": 87, "surface": "transit method", "qid": "Q2069919", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-04", "text": "Galileo used his telescope to discover the four largest moons of Jupiter, now called the Galilean moons.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 7, "surface": "Galileo", "qid": "Q307", "ambiguous": true, "note": "Galileo Galilei: 'his telescope'; not the NASA spacecraft", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 65, "end": 72, "surface": "Jupiter", "qid": "Q319", "ambiguous": true, "note": "planet, not the Roman god", "wbs_rank": 5, "rest_rank": 3, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 89, "end": 103, "surface": "Galilean moons", "qid": "Q3203", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-05", "text": "NASA's Galileo orbited Jupiter for eight years before it was deliberately plunged into the planet in 2003.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 4, "surface": "NASA", "qid": "Q23548", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 2, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 7, "end": 14, "surface": "Galileo", "qid": "Q105425030", "ambiguous": true, "note": "the NASA spacecraft, not Galileo Galilei; the programme is Galileo mission Q182008", "wbs_rank": 15, "rest_rank": 11, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 23, "end": 30, "surface": "Jupiter", "qid": "Q319", "ambiguous": true, "note": "planet, not the Roman god", "wbs_rank": 5, "rest_rank": 3, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-06", "text": "CODATA works with the World Data System, another body of the International Science Council, to promote open science and the FAIR principles.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 6, "surface": "CODATA", "qid": "Q735279", "ambiguous": false, "note": "label 'Committee on Data for Science and Technology'", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 22, "end": 39, "surface": "World Data System", "qid": "Q17102557", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 61, "end": 90, "surface": "International Science Council", "qid": "Q57587699", "ambiguous": false, "note": "Q131906054 is a near-duplicate item without description", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 103, "end": 115, "surface": "open science", "qid": "Q309823", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 124, "end": 139, "surface": "FAIR principles", "qid": "Q29032648", "ambiguous": false, "note": "item labelled 'FAIR data' (the principles)", "wbs_rank": null, "rest_rank": null, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": false, "in_rest20": false}]}
{"id": "test-07", "text": "Darwin's voyage on HMS Beagle to the Galápagos Islands shaped the theory of natural selection he later set out in On the Origin of Species.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 6, "surface": "Darwin", "qid": "Q1035", "ambiguous": true, "note": "Charles Darwin: Beagle, natural selection; not the Australian city", "wbs_rank": 4, "rest_rank": 2, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 19, "end": 29, "surface": "HMS Beagle", "qid": "Q35926", "ambiguous": true, "note": "the 1820 brig-sloop, not the other Royal Navy ships named Beagle", "wbs_rank": 9, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 37, "end": 54, "surface": "Galápagos Islands", "qid": "Q38095", "ambiguous": false, "note": "the archipelago, not the UNESCO site item Q117188122", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 76, "end": 93, "surface": "natural selection", "qid": "Q43478", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 3, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 114, "end": 138, "surface": "On the Origin of Species", "qid": "Q20124", "ambiguous": false, "note": "the work, not a specific edition", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-08", "text": "At the Oakhurst Isotope Facility, the radioactivity of the sample was measured at 3 curies, a unit since replaced by the becquerel in the International System of Units.", "mentions": [{"start": 7, "end": 32, "surface": "Oakhurst Isotope Facility", "qid": null, "ambiguous": false, "note": "NIL: invented facility"}, {"start": 38, "end": 51, "surface": "radioactivity", "qid": "Q11448", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 84, "end": 90, "surface": "curies", "qid": "Q229354", "ambiguous": true, "note": "unit: '3 curies', 'a unit'; popular reading (Marie Curie) is wrong", "wbs_rank": null, "rest_rank": null, "api_top1": false, "in_wbs20": false, "in_rest20": false}, {"start": 121, "end": 130, "surface": "becquerel", "qid": "Q102573", "ambiguous": true, "note": "SI unit, not Henri Becquerel", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 138, "end": 167, "surface": "International System of Units", "qid": "Q12457", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
{"id": "test-09", "text": "DSpace, the repository software used by many universities, is written in Java and exposes its metadata through OAI-PMH.", "mentions": [{"start": 0, "end": 6, "surface": "DSpace", "qid": "Q595786", "ambiguous": false, "note": "repository software, not dSPACE GmbH", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 73, "end": 77, "surface": "Java", "qid": "Q251", "ambiguous": true, "note": "programming language: 'written in'; not the island", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}, {"start": 111, "end": 118, "surface": "OAI-PMH", "qid": "Q2430433", "ambiguous": false, "note": "", "wbs_rank": 1, "rest_rank": 1, "api_top1": true, "in_wbs20": true, "in_rest20": true}]}
